{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implementation record — the unobtainable-signer-key call, and the mutation results\n\nWorktree `c21f-opcert-env` off `origin/main` `ae4f6c1a07a9`. No `--force`, no gate override.\n\n### The decision the task left open: what to do when the signer's public key cannot be obtained\n\nThe prior policy refused every foreign principal because \"there is no key to verify with\". That\npremise is mechanically wrong: an **SSHSIG blob carries the public half it was made with**\n(`MAGIC || version || publickey || namespace || reserved || hash_alg || signature`). So an\nunpinned signer's signature *can* be checked — fully, via the ordinary `ssh-keygen -Y verify`\npath, which validates the signature, the namespace, and the principal binding. What it does not\nestablish is that the key belongs to a *known* environment.\n\n**Chosen: accept on self-consistency, and make the weaker basis VISIBLE.** Results now carry a\n`trust_basis` field naming which key certified:\n\n| `trust_basis` | key used |\n|---|---|\n| `own_key` | this environment's own op-cert key |\n| `pinned_environment` | a key pinned for the signer in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` |\n| `envelope_key` | the signer's own key, read out of the SSHSIG blob |\n\nRationale for accepting rather than reporting a distinct *verdict*: the schema's invariant\n`verified is true iff certified` is relied on by every gate consumer, and a new verdict value would\nhave meant auditing each of them for a case that, under current policy, is meant to pass. A\nseparate, always-present, enum-typed field keeps the acceptance loud without a compatibility\nbreak — and `verified: true` is honest here, because a real cryptographic check ran. This is NOT\n`-Y check-novalidate`, which the codebase still never uses for authorization.\n\nWhere **nothing** can be checked — no own key, nothing pinned, and no parsable key in the envelope\n— the result is `foreign_key` with a reason naming exactly that, plus `trust_basis: null`. `verified`\nis never widened to cover a case where nothing was verified.\n\n`docs/plan-review-gate.md`'s \"no coherent middle where signatures are checked but unpinned signers\nare waved through\" is quoted in situ and explicitly marked as overridden, so the trade-off it names\nstays on the record rather than vanishing.\n\n### Verification discipline\n\n**RED first.** All 7 initial cases failed on the `foreign_key` short-circuit before the fix, with\nthe exact reported message (`op-cert signed by a different environment (principal '9f1c8e42-…';\nthis environment is 'b8a354e0-…')`). Cross-environment cases are built for real — a distinct\nEd25519 keypair bound under a distinct principal via `bound_signer` — never by mocking the\ncomparison away, and every acceptance case first asserts `_own_principal(store) == ENV_B != ENV_A`\nso it cannot be vacuously satisfied by a same-environment attestation. The claim-gate case asserts\nthe gate actually reached a verdict rather than bailing out on store freshness.\n\n**Mutation results** (each seed asserted to apply exactly once; return codes checked, not output):\n\n| # | mutant | killed by |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | restore the unconditional foreign-principal short-circuit | 8 of 10 fail |\n| M2 | `_required_environment` always returns `None` | 2 fail (both enforcement directions) |\n| M3 | do not withhold the envelope-key fallback under enforcement | 1 fails |\n| M4 | pinned keyring lookup always empty | 4 fail |\n| M5 | `trust_basis` never surfaced | 6 fail |\n| M6 | `embedded_public_key` always `None` | 3 fail |\n| M7 | `embedded_public_key` parses at the wrong offset | 3 fail |\n\nBaseline restored: 10/10 green.\n\n**Negative controls** (this is the part that proves only identity was loosened):\n\n- **Forged content, cross-environment** — the SIGNED payload rewritten to claim an extra attested\n  step while the real signature is kept. Still `mismatch`, and `trust_basis == \"envelope_key\"`\n  anchors that the scheme actually ran and rejected it rather than the cert being refused earlier\n  for want of a key.\n- **Forged content, pinned signer** — same, with the environment pinned. Still `mismatch`.\n- **Key substitution under a pinned signer** — re-signed with an attacker keypair while still\n  claiming environment A. Still `mismatch`. This is precisely what pinning buys over the envelope's\n  own key, and what the deferred trusted-set feature restores everywhere.\n- **Unparsable signature blob** — `foreign_key`, `trust_basis: null`, reason \"no public key is\n  obtainable\".\n\n### Opt-in enforcement still works when the keys ARE set\n\nTwo tests with `verify.require_environment` + `verify.opcert_enforce_since` both written to\n`rebar.toml` (the runbook sets them together), each asserting the composed config really carries\nthem before asserting behaviour:\n\n- required environment ≠ the cert's signer → `foreign_key`, reason naming the required environment;\n- required environment = the cert's signer, pinned → `certified` with\n  `trust_basis == \"pinned_environment\"` (still not this environment — enforcement is about the\n  REQUIRED environment, not the local one);\n- required environment = the cert's signer but NOT pinned → `foreign_key`. Under enforcement the\n  envelope-key fallback is deliberately withheld: enforcement must bind to the pinned key, never to\n  a key the certificate supplies about itself.\n\n`infra/runbooks/mcp-opcert-enforcement-flip.md` now warns that the flip tightens the local\nclaim/close path too, so an operator pins the environment their agents actually run `review-plan`\nin.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787962473663879492,"uuid":"389234cb-da2d-4d85-8dc3-513c814d8ea2"}